Lumped capacity, Biot number and response time (@th-lumped: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer, #source "../sources/A-Heat-Transfer-Textbook-Lienhard.lingua#r_7TKQ2D5YRNQ8XVHXNJQDRANWKD", #source "../sources/Differential-Equations-MIT-18-03SC.lingua#r_1VJHPM3DQ0PWYNFYKD11EKGCZE", #source "../../../inquiry/metrology/applications/Temperature-and-thermal-response.lingua#r_480AQX951V9NFA6AB86KJA1QZD", #thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer "Semi-infinite-conduction-diffusion-length-and-similarity.lingua#r_3H9JEH6W8JEQXVRSM8Q4JMX7YW", #materials-engineering "../../../engineering/materials-engineering/processing/Atmosphere-contamination-and-state-before-forming.lingua#r_145ZDZQCRMWM7ENS68BV59A671", #clean-manufacturing "../../../production/clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control/facilities/A-room-temperature-response-includes-more-than-air-capacity.lingua#r_4JCTRNZQ1SZ37NEYQ3QYDMN9VM") { r_57VKB47NKFZ5FDGMVK9RAQ0WJ3
**Read:** [A Heat Transfer Textbook — Lienhard and Lienhard](../sources/A-Heat-Transfer-Textbook-Lienhard.lingua#r_7TKQ2D5YRNQ8XVHXNJQDRANWKD), complete lumped/transient analysis; [Differential Equations — MIT 18.03SC](../sources/Differential-Equations-MIT-18-03SC.lingua#r_1VJHPM3DQ0PWYNFYKD11EKGCZE), first-order linear equations and fitting interpretation; [Temperature of what, when, and under which thermal conditions?](../../../inquiry/metrology/applications/Temperature-and-thermal-response.lingua#r_480AQX951V9NFA6AB86KJA1QZD), measurement response and thermal conditions.

The lumped-capacity approximation treats an object's temperature as spatially uniform while it exchanges heat. Compare internal conduction resistance with external transfer resistance using a defined characteristic length and Biot number. A familiar small-Biot rule is an approximation criterion tied to geometry and the desired error, not a proof for every measurement.

For constant capacity C and conductance G to a fixed ambient, T−T∞ decays exponentially with time constant C/G. Derive the solution and total transferred energy. With radiation, phase change or variable properties, a single exponential may no longer apply.

Compare the lumped prediction with a spatially resolved analytical or numerical solution as Biot number changes. Evaluate surface, centre and average temperature separately. A thermocouple response experiment also requires a sensor model; agreement of two fitted time constants does not establish spatial uniformity.

**Continue:** [Semi-infinite conduction, diffusion length and similarity](Semi-infinite-conduction-diffusion-length-and-similarity.lingua#r_3H9JEH6W8JEQXVRSM8Q4JMX7YW).

